+## INNH-10 (Quarry side, 2026-10-31, built dark; depth only, none is a fix loop)
+
+- INNH-10 | The gRPC validator cannot carry `key_id` until the Model Plane owner picks a field number for `ValidateGrantResponse.key_id` (ask MP-2) and Quarry vendors `proto/browser.proto`. Until then every key run on a gRPC-validated edge (all of production) is refused `api_key_grant_owner_mismatch`: fail closed on purpose, but a first live test will look like a bug. RUNBOOKS says so.
+- INNH-10 | Added beyond the design: `api_key_budget_not_enforced` (a key start is refused unless `QUARRY_EDGE__BUDGET_ENFORCEMENT=on`) and `api_key_grant_hosts_required`. Reason: in `shadow` (the default) the budget gate only records, so a grant's budget would bound nothing for a leaked key; an empty or non-canonical host list is its own reason because the design named none. Owner may prefer a boot-time refusal for the budget mode instead of a per-start one.
+- INNH-10 | A key grant is refused for every non-key presenter, the owner's own session included (`api_key_grant_owner_mismatch`), at start and on every later step (`browser grant belongs to an API key`). The design only required the key-side bind; this closes "a leaked grant id plus the owner's session" and the known gap (a service principal is not bound to the grant owner, `start_run_grants.rs:397`) for key grants specifically. The gap for ordinary grants stays pinned and open.
+- INNH-10 | Audit of the 25 `is_service()` branches: reachable from the nine allowed routes were `derive_initiator` (key is now always an agent), the run class (key is Agent), `web_policy_edge::principal` (a key was `Principal::Human`, which would have given a key a person's web-policy latitude; now Autonomous) and `usage_attrs::surface_for` (key is `Api`, correct). Every other branch (attach, handoff, job lifecycle, search, scrape actions, schedules, profile guard) sits behind routes the fence refuses. The fence is therefore the control for them: when a route is ever added to the allow-list, re-audit that route's `is_service()` and `is_person()` uses first.
+- INNH-10 | `RunLimits.max_runs_per_key` counts a key's live runs by `RunLive.actor_id == apikey:<key_id>` inside the organization. It is a count of runs in this edge process; with more than one edge replica the cap is per replica (same as every other run cap).
+- INNH-10 | Non-ZDR key runs still write a durable checkpoint and receipts under actor `apikey:<key_id>` (like any agent run). Resume is refused for keys, so the checkpoint is only a record; `run_gone_err` can still answer a key's step on a swept run with the person's `resumable: true` hint. Cosmetic, but the hint is wrong for a key.
+- INNH-10 | Usage events for a key run carry the actor `apikey:<key_id>` where a person's carry a user id. Control Plane metering decides whether that is a valid user dimension (OD-17).
+- INNH-10 | Quarry has no durable audit stream; the Quarry-side trail is the per-step receipt (names the key) plus `agent.run.started` / `agent.run.refused` log lines with ids only. A durable stream for key-driven starts and refusals is a separate item.
+- INNH-10 | The events stream ends at the token's `exp` (not `exp` plus the verifier's 30 s leeway). A key client that holds a stream open must reconnect with a fresh token; `Last-Event-ID` replay covers the gap.
+- INNH-10 | Key token `acting_user_id` is trusted as the owner: Quarry cannot check the user is still an active member of the key's org (Auth Core's exchange does, ask CP-1). A grant whose `owner_id` was issued before the user left the org still validates until the broker revokes it on `api_key_revoked` (CP-3/MP-2).
