+## INNH-10 review (2026-10-31, the one Opus security review, design and build together, read-only review of a7ceb6da8, bf9f7631f, c507c9e7f, c93b6299a)
+
+Checked: the gap, flag-off behaviour, the new principal and how the edge recognises it, the route fence on both authenticated routers (layer order require_auth > mark_floor > fence > service scope), grant binding at start and on every step and tab, org and key_id binding, ZDR including the token floor, the per-key cap, the event-stream deadline, logs and error text, openapi / CONTRACTS / ERROR_REASONS / RUNBOOKS against the code, and the tests. Node docs tests run on the host: 63 tests, 60 pass, 3 fail (the same 3 base failures the builder names: `/v1/audit/page` undocumented, route count 109 vs 108, generated SDK internal files). Rust not run (as instructed). Auth-core, model-gateway and the Verevon gateway grepped: nothing mints `principal_type: api_key` or a `key_id` claim today (auth-core's `issuePlaneToken` types are `user | service` only). No blocker found in the Quarry side: every new path fails closed, the fence is a closed allow-list matched on method and raw path, the key actor `apikey:<key_id>` gives correct run scoping on all nine routes (each handler checks `caller_owns_live_run` / `owned_by`, durable receipts are actor-scoped), and the gRPC path cannot admit a key run at all until MP-2. Everything below is depth.
+
+- INNH-10 | review | Flag off is not literally byte for byte, only for every token minted today. Differences: (1) a token with `principal_type: api_key` was a 401 (unknown principal), it is now a valid principal refused 403 `api_key_agent_runs_disabled`; (2) a user or service token that carries a `key_id` claim was accepted (unknown claims are ignored), it is now a 401 (`auth.rs` user/service arms); (3) the edge now refuses to boot on an unreadable `QUARRY_EDGE__API_KEY_AGENT_RUNS` or `QUARRY_EDGE__MAX_CONCURRENT_RUNS_PER_KEY=0`. No minter emits either claim shape, so nothing deployed changes. Fix the wording of the status file and CONTRACTS ("behaviour is exactly what it was") to say "for every token minted today", or keep the old 401 for key tokens while the switch is off (move the switch check into `validate_and_normalize_principal`); the 403 also tells a prober the edge knows the type, harmless.
+- INNH-10 | review | Quarry does not bound the key token's lifetime. The revocation story (design section 7, RUNBOOKS kill switches "within the token's life, at most two minutes", CONTRACTS `exp` row) rests entirely on Auth Core minting at most 120 s; `Claims` has no `iat` and Quarry checks only `exp`. A CP-1 mistake (for example reusing the ordinary plane-token TTL) silently turns the 2-minute kill into the full TTL, and the event stream follows `exp`. Cheap fail-closed fix for the arm in `validate_and_normalize_principal` or the fence: refuse an `api_key` token whose `exp - now` exceeds 120 s plus the leeway (a named reason, e.g. `api_key_token_ttl_exceeded`), and say so in CONTRACTS. `jti` is not checked either (pure bearer within its life; replay from a gateway log leak works for its lifetime; sender binding is OD-15).
+- INNH-10 | review | A key grant backs any number of runs. One grant id can start up to `MAX_CONCURRENT_RUNS_PER_KEY` concurrent runs and an unlimited number of sequential ones within the grant's TTL, and each run snapshots the FULL grant budget (`grant_budget` at `lifecycle.rs` start, applied per run). So the grant budget bounds one run, not the grant; the design's "spend per run" row says this, but its leaked-key paragraph ("each within the key's budget ceiling") reads as if the grant were the spend unit. Since the gateway composes one grant per run (OD-3), make key grants single-run: Quarry records the grant id on start and refuses a second start under the same key grant (`api_key_grant_in_use` or reuse `api_key_grant_kind_refused`), or the broker consumes the grant on first validate for kind session+key_id. Owner decision on whether the ceiling is per run or per grant (OD-10).
+- INNH-10 | review | Budget claim "bounds something" means any ONE of `micro_usd`, `max_steps`, `max_runtime_s` (`GrantBudget::is_set`, `grant_validator.rs:112`). Design section 1 makes all three mandatory for a key grant; the U2 text says only "budget claim set". A grant with only `micro_usd` relies on the flat action-cost table to bound steps and time. Fail-closed fix: for key grants require `max_steps > 0` and `max_runtime_s > 0` (and `micro_usd > 0` if the cost table bills browser actions), else `api_key_grant_budget_required`.
+- INNH-10 | review | A key run outlives its grant. The lease TTL is the request's `constraints.max_runtime_s` (default 120, `lifecycle.rs:399`), not the grant's remaining life or budget runtime; after the grant expires every step and new tab fails, but the browser lease and the per-key slot stay until the idle sweeper or the backstop. The event stream's viewer guard (`live.rs:294`) keeps the run from idle expiry, so a key that reconnects the stream every token life holds a browser (and one of its two slots) until the agent max-TTL backstop with no grant at all. Bounded (2 runs, backstop), but cheap to close: clamp `ttl_s` for a key run to `min(grant absolute_expires_at, grant max_runtime_s)` and finish the run when per-step revalidation says the grant is inactive.
+- INNH-10 | review | A service delegate can attach to a key's run. `attach_run` (`attach.rs:172`) checks only the org, a grant and non-ZDR; it does not refuse a run whose `key_id` is set, so with `QUARRY_EDGE__ATTACH` on a Model Plane service in the same org can attach and drive the key's browser (non-sensitive actions only, because admission refuses sensitive actions for the whole entry, and on the key's hosts). Not a key escalation, but a cross-principal path the design does not name (it lists attach only as refused FOR keys). Refuse attach to key runs (`attach_key_run_refused`) until a design says otherwise; MP-1's "refuse DeriveRunGrant for api_key" does not cover it because the deriving caller is the service.
+- INNH-10 | review | Enable-order trap on gRPC edges (deviation 2 is not effective there yet). The gRPC mapping always yields `key_id: ""`, so if the broker starts issuing key grants (MP-2) before Quarry vendors the field, a key grant presented by a NON-key caller is not recognised as a key grant: the key owner's own session passes `bind_validated_grant` (owner match) and any org service with `browser:execute` passes the open service-owner gap (`start_run_grants.rs:397`). Low risk (the owner's own powers; trusted services), but RUNBOOKS should make "vendor `ValidateGrantResponse.key_id` into Quarry" a precondition of `BROWSER_BROKER_KEY_GRANTS_ENABLED`, not only of the Quarry switch. A distinct grant kind for key grants (e.g. `api_key_session`) would make older consumers refuse them structurally; worth asking MP-2.
+- INNH-10 | review | The only validator that can admit a key run today is the HTTP shim, and it is org-blind (`HttpGrantValidator` does not override `validate_for_org`, `grant_validator.rs:223-229` drops the org) and `GrantValidation` carries no org to compare. Cross-org safety for key runs on such an edge rests on key ids being globally unique and the broker stamping them correctly. Production refuses HTTP-only, so this is dev; still, consider refusing boot with the switch on and only the HTTP validator in every environment, or adding the grant's org to `GrantValidation` and comparing it for key runs.
+- INNH-10 | review | ZDR matrix wording: the `agent_run_api_key` basis (`zdr_matrix.rs:461`) says "so a key run never reads or writes a durable profile or checkpoint of its own". False for a non-ZDR key run: `start_run` writes a durable checkpoint for every non-ZDR run (`lifecycle.rs:779`), and steps keep writing it (the builder's own depth note above says so). The ZDR treatment itself is right (under ZDR nothing is written; Honored/Enforced is accurate). Reword to "never resumes from or attaches a durable profile or checkpoint; a non-ZDR key run's checkpoint is written like any agent run's and cannot be resumed by anyone", regenerate the golden and the `.sha256`.
+- INNH-10 | review | ZDR checked: the key token's `zdr` counts as a floor (`zdr_floor`, `!is_service()`), pinned by `api_key_token_zdr_floor_counts` and `a_zdr_key_token_makes_a_zdr_run_and_a_zdr_grant_needs_one`; a ZDR grant cannot back a non-ZDR run; resume (the only path that reads a checkpoint back) is refused before any read; profiles refused. Quarry sends nothing to a model on these routes. Gap left: none in Quarry; the floor is only as good as CP-1's "org posture OR key flag" (raise-only) rule, which nothing in Quarry can check.
+- INNH-10 | review | Audit visibility: receipts of a key's runs are readable only with that key's own token (actor scoping), so the key's owner and the org's admins have no Quarry route to see what a key did, and after the key is deleted or rotated the receipts are orphaned. With no durable Quarry audit stream, a leaked-key investigation has only the broker's grant log and log lines. Add an owner/admin read path keyed by `acting_user_id` or the durable stream (separate item), before keys go live.
+- INNH-10 | review | `profile_scope` from the body is still accepted for a key (`org_shared`, `user_private`); harmless today (no profile id, no persistence, fresh profile, the owner stamp is skipped as not persistent), but refuse a non-default scope for keys so "a key never has a profile" is structural, not a consequence of three other checks.
+- INNH-10 | review | Metering: usage events carry actor `apikey:<key_id>`. If the Control Plane consumer validates the actor as a user id and drops or dead-letters unknown shapes, key-driven usage goes unbilled and unquota'd, which makes free-tier abuse a security question, not only attribution (OD-17). Check the consumer's behaviour before the switch is on anywhere metered.
+- INNH-10 | review | Tests are meaningful: real router, real RS256 verification, the closed allow-list tested both ways (every allowed path refuses every other method; 40 refused paths incl. traversal, trailing slash, `%2e%2e`), the grant bind in every direction including the owner's own session and a service, revocation / swap / widening on the next step and tab, sensitive actions with the broker never asked about the approval, the cap with a freed slot. Missing: a key variant of the foreign-org grant test (`start_run_grants.rs:507`; the FakeBroker ignores the org), and tests for the attach and lease-TTL paths above.
+- INNH-10 | review | Logs and errors: the fence and start refusals log reason, key id and org only; refusal messages are fixed sentences per reason; no host, token, key or URL in any new line or body. `agent.run.started` carries grant id (`z_` ids under ZDR are ids, fine). Nothing to fix.
+- INNH-10 | review | Owner decisions, security-critical: OD-1 (principal model: the new type is what makes every unknown branch fail closed; D1a would be fail-open), OD-2 (only the gateway may exchange: otherwise a leaked key mints tokens directly), OD-4 (user-keys only: org-keys have no owner to bind), OD-5 (explicit `browser:agent`, who may grant it), OD-6 (mandatory host ceiling: the only SSRF bound beyond broker policy), OD-7 (no open web), OD-8 (no sensitive actions), OD-9 (token and grant lifetimes: the whole revocation story; see the TTL finding), OD-11 (per-key concurrency and exchange rate), OD-12 (ZDR orgs and the raise-only key flag), OD-14 (no resume or persistent profile: cookie theft), OD-15 (key lifetime, IP allow-list: the leaked-key window), OD-16 (event revoke vs per-validate check: revocation latency), new (a) budget-mode refusal per start vs at boot (either is safe; boot is clearer), new (b) the service-principal owner gap for ordinary grants (a confused-deputy path for every service in an org; recommend closing it as its own item), and the new question of single-run key grants above. Cost/abuse-critical: OD-10 (budget ceiling, per run or per grant), OD-17 (metering must not drop key usage). Product only: OD-3 (who composes; security-neutral if the gateway strips caller headers as GW-1 says), OD-13 (artifacts route, excluded either way in v1).
+- INNH-10 | review | Not verified by me: no Rust run (the 760 lib, 7 fence and 15 key-run tests are the builder's counts), nothing against a stack; the CP/MP/GW halves do not exist, so the chain's real security (exchange auth, broker key_id stamping, revoke-on-event, gateway header stripping) is unreviewed code that is not written yet. Each ask (CP-1..4, MP-1..3, GW-1) needs its own review when built.
