+## 2026-11-02 - Quarry W2 Model Plane halves: browser planner, run trace, extract lane, key grants, scrub (branch mp-w2w3)
+
+**Status: implemented behind default-off switches (unless stated), tested in process only; nothing live-proven.** One entry for the Model Plane rows of the Quarry W2 wave, built on branch `mp-w2w3` (status table: `.plan-work/quarry-w1w2/MP-W2W3-STATUS.md`). Each paragraph is its own decision; supersede them one by one.
+
+**WS10.S2 envelope allocation.** `model_plane.v1.Event` gains `producer_seq = 21`, `agent = 22` (`AgentRef`), `origin = 23` (`RunOrigin`), `run_id = 24`, `request_id = 25`. The plan's 14-18 was refused by `buf breaking` (shrinking the published `reserved 14 to 20` is `RESERVED_MESSAGE_NO_DELETE`), so the reservation stays and 26+ is free. `EventType` 150-156 are the Quarry browser trace events, 160 is `BROWSER_ATTACH_SHARED_JAR` (WS8.G); 157-159 and 161+ stay open; pause and resume reuse 142/143 with a `reason`. The JSON twins (`mp-events::Envelope`, Go `pkg/envelope`) carry the five fields optional and omitted when empty; trace producers always write `zdr` (an absent key reads as false). The Rust twin keeps `origin` a string so an unknown future origin does not fail an old consumer's whole decode. Golden: `api/contracts/run-trace-envelope.golden.json`. Persistence (WS10.S6) is session-core `run_trace_events` (migration 0086; 0074-0085 are taken on branch `space`), read through `EventLog.QueryEvents` and `GET /v1/runs/:run_id/trace` (404 for not-found and permission-denied alike).
+
+**C0 Quarry client contract.** The execution-core agent client sends optional `policy`, `trace` and `initiated_by` fields and `x-request-id`; a non-2xx Quarry envelope parses to `AgentClientError::Typed`, and `classify_quarry_error` is the one place that decides Failed, recoverable or resource-exhausted.
+
+**WS11 browser planner (S0-S7).** Per-run bounds come from `PlanCeilings` (env default and ceiling; the grant claim only tightens). Quarry 402 `BUDGET_EXHAUSTED` and 429 end a browser run as resource-exhausted (`BrowserResourceLimit::QuarryBudget` / `QuarryBackpressure`), never Failed and never retried; the planner token budget is separate from Quarry spend (R-9 default). Durable planner selection is a deployment mode plus an org flag (`PlannerPolicy`; interim carrier an org-core quota row until F-t2-022); `default_on` needs the `QUARRY_BROWSER_AGENT_DEFAULT_ON_ACK` boot guard. Two guards default ON because they only tighten, against the "new behaviour defaults off" rule: the web-taint memory guard (`EXECUTION_CORE_EXTERNAL_CONTENT_GUARD`, `SESSION_CORE_DREAMING_SKIP_EXTERNAL_CONTENT`; `ConnectorContent` is not taint, plan default), and the step/runtime ceilings (a caller asking for more than 100 steps or 900 s is clamped). Connector-first routing guidance (S6) defaults OFF, against the spec's proposed default on. Both services classify external content through `mp_content_safety::TrustClass`, never a hand-mirrored tool list.
+
+**WS7.L policy ask and WS10.S4 origin.** A model-driven browser run sends only a tighten-only `RequestAsk` (`disable`, `deny_domains`, `allow_domains`) on `POST /v1/agent/runs`; plan mode is the gateway's expansion into four disables. A gateway with `MODEL_GATEWAY_BROWSER_POLICY_ASK` off REFUSES a start that carries an ask (400), because dropping a restriction silently is worse than failing. Quarry policy denials render as a Blocked observation with fixed text and end the run (`Failed` + summary, plan default; the `PermissionDenied` abort reason is not built), never an approval. Run origin is a label (USER, SCHEDULE, AGENT, WEBHOOK, API) mapped from a closed set of run sources; the request id travels on every Quarry call.
+
+**WS8.G attach mode.** A browser run start with `attach_session_id` (gateway switch `MODEL_GATEWAY_BROWSER_ATTACH`, default off) derives an `attach` grant (purpose `attach`, `resource_id` = the session id, no budget claim, so it inherits the parent's) with the caller's own bearer before any thread or run write, only from a non-ZDR `session` or `human_browse` parent and with explicit hosts (at most eight). execution-core then never acquires a lease: it steps as a delegate on the person's Quarry run (`initiated_by: agent`, the Model run id as advisory `ai_run_id`), treats Quarry's 409 `USER_TOOK_OVER` as a pause, and releases the attachment on `Completed` and aborts it on every other exit (D6b). Give-back is detected with a passive probe step once a second, because Quarry's delegate tab list has no `holder`; a refused probe is free. The take-over wait is 600 s (D5), capped by the plan's and the attachment's runtime left. The shared-jar audit event (`EVENT_TYPE_BROWSER_ATTACH_SHARED_JAR` = 160, oneof arm 30; ids only) is emitted once per attachment (D4, plan default; D8-6 still open for GA); pause and resume carry a `reason` (field 3). An attach grant lifts the browser high-risk `ask` on the same terms as a run grant. Open: the broker caps an attach grant at 15 minutes and renewal is not wired, so a longer attached run loses its grant mid-run.
+
+**WS8.J, WS9.E, WS12.S4b.** A completed attached browser run marks its agent tab (default `deliverable`; `none` or `handoff` via `tab_mark_on_complete`, gateway-validated and only with `attach_session_id`) right before releasing the attachment; every other exit marks nothing, and a failed mark never blocks the release. The `browser.mark_tab` model tool is deferred (D7); Quarry's mark route is not built yet. `browser.console` reads a run's console through Quarry's devtools route (`category=console`, Quarry's camelCase query) and returns at most 100 entries, 16 KB and 500 chars per text of `{sequence, tab_id, level, text}` only, scrubbed with the Live set; it is a read under `cap.browser.open` (capability-core migration 0034) and rides `EXECUTION_DO_BROWSER_ENABLED`. The planner prompt, the `observation_received` event and the model-written done answer are scrubbed at the boundary with the `mp-scrub` Live set, never inside `observation_from_wire`, so stop criteria and the risk gate read the raw observation. Quarry's redaction stamp is read leniently (only `rule_version` is kept); `EXECUTION_CORE_REQUIRE_QUARRY_REDACTION_STAMP=1` refuses an observation without one, off it is logged once per run.
+
+**WS16.S0 DR-15.1 (status `proposed`; trigger: WS16.S6 tier E2 passes).** Quarry reaches the Model Plane through one allowlisted, org-bound, metered machine lane: `service:quarry-edge` may call `POST /v1/ai/chat` and only the routes in `SERVICE_ROUTE_GRANTS` (golden-pinned in `api/contracts/model-gateway-service-routes.json`; rows may carry a default-off `flag`, E3.6-8). Quarry sends the run's ZDR flag explicitly; a non-ZDR call is metered to cost-core under the org (`MODEL_GATEWAY_AI_CHAT_METERING`, default off until the first live extraction) and a service draws from its own `<org>|svc:<service>` rate bucket. Rejected: opening `/v1/invoke` to services (it writes page text to a durable thread, hard-codes sampling, refuses `max_cost_usd` under ZDR); a gateway-held inference principal; flipping `model-invoke`, `model-stream`, `model-audio` to granted. Undecided: AI-format pricing, whether ZDR extraction is ledgered (default: nothing published), org `injection_defense` and required PII redaction on this lane.
+
+**WS16.S8 interim extract route.** The Model-proposed extract route lives in `model-gateway/src/web_extraction.rs` and serves both gRPC `ExtractStructured` and the chat tool `extract_structured` (default off, `MODEL_GATEWAY_EXTRACT_TOOL`). The answer is checked, not trusted: schema validation with one page-free repair for json mode; verbatim grounding for question and highlights. Interim until Quarry's own extract grant (WS16.S2-S7) exists; Quarry's route stays the target. The schema corpus is the Model Plane's own until Quarry publishes a byte-stable one.
+
+**INNH-10 API-key principal (MP-1 to MP-3).** `authctx` gains an opt-in `api_key` principal (`Config.AcceptAPIKey`; only browser-broker opts in). Rejected: teaching every authctx consumer the type (six services switch on `PrincipalType == user` with non-user meaning scoped service; opt-in keeps them fail-closed with no edit). The broker's `OwnerFilter` no longer returns the org-wide filter for an unknown principal type (fixed first, 4501c9a85). model-gateway admits an `api_key` principal on exactly `POST /v1/browser/key-grants` and `DELETE /v1/browser/key-grants/:grant_id` (allow-list in `authorize_principal_route`, proven over the source route table), with ingress audience `model-gateway` (CP-1 adds it) plus the key's own `aud=browser-broker` bearer as a distinct extractor type. Rejected: a route-specific verifier accepting the broker-audience token directly as ingress (a second ingress audience policy). Broker key grants carry `key_id` (proto field 60, migration 0005) and are switched off by `BROWSER_BROKER_KEY_GRANTS_ENABLED`.
+
+**WS12.S4 mp-scrub.** The legacy scrubber moved to the shared crate `crates/mp-scrub` (execution-core's `scrub.rs` re-exports it and keeps `scrub_fold`). A Live set (structured credential shapes only) is held to Quarry's authored golden `secret-shapes.v1.json`, vendored byte-identical (cases whose `applies_to` contains `mp`). Live masks at the prompt and event boundary only, never inside `observation_from_wire`. Decided: Live `scrub_url` masks secret query and fragment values (Quarry's Live set does not, its Durable set does) because a URL query token is the commonest page-borne credential. The marker stays `[REDACTED]`; Quarry's typed marker is mapped in the test harness only.
